1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,064
Here is a small app on one virtual machine:

2
00:00:03,064 --> 00:00:07,125
a web server in front,
a frontend and two APIs behind it.

3
00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:10,453
If one of them crashes in the night,
who restarts it?

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:13,943
At two in the morning,
one API container crashes.

5
00:00:14,263 --> 00:00:17,327
The app owner finds out when users complain,

6
00:00:17,327 --> 00:00:21,367
and the operator logs in over SSH
and restarts it by hand.

7
00:00:21,617 --> 00:00:25,226
In lecture 20,
Docker could restart a crashed container,

8
00:00:25,226 --> 00:00:27,095
but only on that one machine:

9
00:00:27,095 --> 00:00:30,961
if the machine itself failed,
the whole site waited for you.

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:33,717
So the app owner asks for several servers.

11
00:00:34,037 --> 00:00:39,051
The operator answers with three machines,
and a backup copy beside each API.

12
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:42,351
Each backup sits
on the same machine as its original.

13
00:00:42,671 --> 00:00:45,767
Name one failure
that still takes that API down,

14
00:00:45,767 --> 00:00:48,025
and say who switches to the backup.

15
00:00:56,867 --> 00:00:58,128
The machine itself:

16
00:00:58,128 --> 00:01:01,646
one failed disk or network
takes both copies at once.

17
00:01:01,966 --> 00:01:06,509
And even when a backup survives,
a person still has to switch over by hand.

18
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:09,822
The lecture wants three jobs done
with nobody awake.

19
00:01:10,142 --> 00:01:12,745
Failover: a working copy takes over.

20
00:01:13,065 --> 00:01:16,094
Load balancing:
requests spread across copies.

21
00:01:16,414 --> 00:01:18,953
Scaling: more copies when traffic grows.

22
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,522
This card comes back each time we see

23
00:01:21,522 --> 00:01:24,597
what does one of these jobs,
and we tick it then.

24
00:01:24,917 --> 00:01:27,221
The tool for all three is Kubernetes.

25
00:01:29,267 --> 00:01:31,246
Kubernetes is an orchestrator:

26
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:34,346
it runs containers
across a cluster of machines

27
00:01:34,346 --> 00:01:36,691
and keeps them the way you declared.

28
00:01:37,011 --> 00:01:41,747
The name is Greek for helmsman,
and Helm, a tool in part 2, borrows it.

29
00:01:41,984 --> 00:01:46,144
On the left, one machine runs
every container on one Docker engine.

30
00:01:46,464 --> 00:01:50,599
On the right, many machines,
called nodes, share the containers,

31
00:01:50,599 --> 00:01:53,120
and one control plane coordinates them.

32
00:01:53,367 --> 00:01:57,451
A word of warning:
here, deployment means a way of deploying,

33
00:01:57,451 --> 00:02:00,130
not the Deployment object we meet later.

34
00:02:00,367 --> 00:02:03,610
In lecture 20 the cheese app ran
on one machine.

35
00:02:03,930 --> 00:02:08,111
Now the same app goes to a cluster,
and the tools gain kubectl.

36
00:02:08,431 --> 00:02:12,660
Our toolbox already has it,
plus Helm and the GKE auth plugin,

37
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:14,639
which the slides do not list.

38
00:02:14,884 --> 00:02:16,010
To follow along:

39
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:20,021
cheese-app-v3 with your secrets folder
beside it, Docker,

40
00:02:20,021 --> 00:02:22,799
and a Google Cloud project with billing.

41
00:02:23,119 --> 00:02:27,449
A cluster costs money by the hour,
so each part ends by deleting it.

42
00:02:27,684 --> 00:02:32,299
The tracker names the README step,
code panels name their file and lines,

43
00:02:32,299 --> 00:02:34,639
and your turn means pause and run it.

44
00:02:34,959 --> 00:02:37,796
A grey tag in the corner
names the lecture slides.

45
00:02:38,034 --> 00:02:41,853
One honest note:
our recording machine has no Google account.

46
00:02:42,173 --> 00:02:44,739
What runs on a machine you own is real;

47
00:02:44,739 --> 00:02:48,029
every Google step stops
at its first honest error,

48
00:02:48,029 --> 00:02:49,938
and we say what yours prints.

49
00:02:50,184 --> 00:02:53,976
We follow main,
the branch the course site links,

50
00:02:53,976 --> 00:02:56,221
at this November 2025 commit.

51
00:02:56,541 --> 00:02:58,482
Git log tells you which one you have.

52
00:02:58,717 --> 00:03:01,512
Part 1 does the lecture's warm-up by hand.

53
00:03:01,832 --> 00:03:04,264
Part 2 deploys the cheese app as code.

54
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:06,893
Let's open the cluster.

55
00:03:07,213 --> 00:03:09,368
The lecture draws it in three steps.

56
00:03:09,688 --> 00:03:13,823
First: one control plane,
with arrows to several worker nodes,

57
00:03:13,823 --> 00:03:16,024
each an ordinary virtual machine.

58
00:03:16,267 --> 00:03:18,294
Second, the two planes get names.

59
00:03:18,614 --> 00:03:20,406
The control plane is the brain.

60
00:03:20,726 --> 00:03:24,544
On GKE, Google runs it,
and you never see its machines.

61
00:03:24,864 --> 00:03:26,959
The workers carry your containers:

62
00:03:26,959 --> 00:03:29,856
for the cheese app,
a pool of one or two nodes.

63
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:31,295
Third, the parts.

64
00:03:31,615 --> 00:03:36,841
The API server is the single entry point;
every other part talks only to it.

65
00:03:37,161 --> 00:03:41,983
Etcd is the memory of the cluster:
its state and configuration.

66
00:03:42,217 --> 00:03:44,734
The scheduler picks a node for each new Pod.

67
00:03:45,054 --> 00:03:46,803
The controllers keep watching

68
00:03:46,803 --> 00:03:50,662
and fix any difference
between what you asked for and what runs;

69
00:03:50,662 --> 00:03:53,737
some of them ask the cloud
for nodes and addresses.

70
00:03:53,983 --> 00:03:58,079
On every worker,
the kubelet is the hands of the API server:

71
00:03:58,079 --> 00:04:00,468
it starts and stops the containers.

72
00:04:00,788 --> 00:04:03,451
kube-proxy routes traffic for Services,

73
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:07,615
and a container runtime runs the containers,
grouped in Pods.

74
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:11,327
A small note:
the slide names Docker as the runtime.

75
00:04:11,647 --> 00:04:16,895
On GKE it is containerd,
line 52 of create_cluster.py.

76
00:04:17,215 --> 00:04:20,821
You still build images with Docker,
and they run unchanged.

77
00:04:21,067 --> 00:04:22,560
Now follow the arrows.

78
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,793
Every kubectl command goes to one place,

79
00:04:25,793 --> 00:04:29,216
the API server,
which stores your wish in etcd.

80
00:04:29,536 --> 00:04:32,480
The scheduler and the kubelets
pick up their work from it.

81
00:04:32,717 --> 00:04:35,540
The lecture gives kubectl one grammar:

82
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:39,032
a command,
a type, an optional name, and flags.

83
00:04:39,352 --> 00:04:40,376
Get nodes.

84
00:04:40,696 --> 00:04:41,696
Get pods.

85
00:04:41,997 --> 00:04:44,727
Get pods, with a flag for every namespace.

86
00:04:44,967 --> 00:04:48,423
When something is stuck,
ask which of these parts is waiting.

87
00:04:48,743 --> 00:04:51,454
Four lamps on this picture
will light up later,

88
00:04:51,454 --> 00:04:53,415
each with a symptom you will meet.

89
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:58,198
The README keeps this warm-up
after the cheese app's deployment.

90
00:04:58,518 --> 00:05:01,296
The lecture does it first, and so do we:

91
00:05:01,296 --> 00:05:04,491
it is the gentlest first contact
with kubectl.

92
00:05:04,733 --> 00:05:07,741
First, enable five APIs in the console.

93
00:05:08,061 --> 00:05:11,901
A small slip: Google Container Registry
has been shut down.

94
00:05:12,221 --> 00:05:14,589
Enable Artifact Registry instead.

95
00:05:14,833 --> 00:05:18,289
Then start the toolbox: from the src folder,

96
00:05:18,289 --> 00:05:21,980
change into deployment and run
docker-shell.sh.

97
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:24,024
As committed, it fails:

98
00:05:24,024 --> 00:05:27,847
404 Not Found from Debian,
then pull access denied.

99
00:05:28,083 --> 00:05:30,869
If you did lecture 20, you have seen this:

100
00:05:30,869 --> 00:05:35,379
the base image is Debian bullseye,
whose security packages are gone.

101
00:05:35,699 --> 00:05:38,686
On Windows there is no docker-shell.bat.

102
00:05:39,006 --> 00:05:40,393
Use WSL.

103
00:05:40,633 --> 00:05:44,238
The fix is the same two one-line edits,
an extra here.

104
00:05:44,558 --> 00:05:46,567
Bullseye becomes bookworm,

105
00:05:46,567 --> 00:05:50,173
and google-cloud-sdk becomes
google-cloud-cli,

106
00:05:50,173 --> 00:05:52,132
with its GKE auth plugin.

107
00:05:52,452 --> 00:05:56,143
Every kubectl call to GKE goes
through that plugin.

108
00:05:56,383 --> 00:06:02,335
As in lecture 20, also put your project
on line 13 of docker-shell.sh.

109
00:06:02,583 --> 00:06:06,423
Run it again:
it builds in about 92 seconds and starts.

110
00:06:06,743 --> 00:06:10,823
The login then fails,
because our key belongs to no account;

111
00:06:10,823 --> 00:06:14,359
with yours,
these lines say activated and logged in.

112
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:19,187
Pause here and start your toolbox;
the rest of this part runs at its prompt.

113
00:06:19,433 --> 00:06:20,926
Next, check the tools.

114
00:06:21,246 --> 00:06:24,148
The gcloud tool
and its auth plugin are there.

115
00:06:24,468 --> 00:06:28,781
Plain kubectl version prints the client,
then connection refused:

116
00:06:28,781 --> 00:06:32,895
there is no cluster yet,
so the server half has nobody to ask.

117
00:06:33,883 --> 00:06:38,059
A small surprise:
the Dockerfile pins one kubectl version,

118
00:06:38,059 --> 00:06:43,099
but Google's own package wins:
a dispatcher that matches each cluster.

119
00:06:43,333 --> 00:06:47,152
Last, check that you are authenticated:
gcloud auth list.

120
00:06:47,472 --> 00:06:49,737
Ours says No credentialed accounts;

121
00:06:49,737 --> 00:06:53,168
yours lists the deployment
service account as active.

122
00:06:54,417 --> 00:06:57,821
Now Create Cluster:
gcloud container clusters

123
00:06:57,821 --> 00:07:01,756
create test-cluster
--num-nodes 2 --zone us-east1-c.

124
00:07:02,076 --> 00:07:04,892
Ours stops at once: no active account.

125
00:07:05,212 --> 00:07:09,073
Pause and run yours;
it costs money every hour it exists.

126
00:07:09,717 --> 00:07:12,537
Yours takes a short wait, prints a table,

127
00:07:12,537 --> 00:07:17,077
and adds an entry to your kubeconfig
so kubectl knows where to go.

128
00:07:17,397 --> 00:07:19,015
The slide makes one node;

129
00:07:19,015 --> 00:07:22,965
the README two, so two copies
can land on different machines.

130
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:28,683
To keep going, we start a small real cluster
on the laptop with a tool called kind:

131
00:07:28,683 --> 00:07:30,731
one control plane, two workers.

132
00:07:31,051 --> 00:07:35,445
Same API server,
same kubectl, same YAML, but no Google.

133
00:07:35,765 --> 00:07:38,176
It is not in the README; don't run it.

134
00:07:38,750 --> 00:07:42,620
Here even the control plane
is a container we can see;

135
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:44,340
on GKE, Google hides it.

136
00:07:44,584 --> 00:07:46,625
Next, look around the console:

137
00:07:46,625 --> 00:07:51,389
Kubernetes Engine, your cluster,
its nodes, and the Pods on each node.

138
00:07:51,709 --> 00:07:55,613
In Compute Engine,
your nodes are ordinary virtual machines.

139
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:58,505
Then try some kubectl commands.

140
00:07:58,825 --> 00:08:00,999
First an extra, kubectl version:

141
00:08:00,999 --> 00:08:05,417
the dispatcher picked a client
that matches the server's version.

142
00:08:05,967 --> 00:08:07,796
The first README command,

143
00:08:07,796 --> 00:08:11,236
get all,
shows only a service named kubernetes,

144
00:08:11,236 --> 00:08:13,690
the door to the API server itself.

145
00:08:14,010 --> 00:08:16,592
We are looking
in the namespace called default;

146
00:08:16,592 --> 00:08:17,636
more on that later.

147
00:08:18,184 --> 00:08:21,113
With all namespaces, kube-system appears,

148
00:08:21,113 --> 00:08:24,542
and here you can spot
the control plane by name:

149
00:08:24,542 --> 00:08:28,829
etcd, the API server,
the controller manager, the scheduler.

150
00:08:29,149 --> 00:08:31,026
On GKE those stay hidden.

151
00:08:31,267 --> 00:08:34,385
componentstatuses prints
a deprecation warning,

152
00:08:34,385 --> 00:08:37,304
which is expected, then three healthy lines.

153
00:08:37,624 --> 00:08:41,103
Get nodes is the better check:
three nodes ready here;

154
00:08:41,103 --> 00:08:44,003
yours shows two workers
and no control plane.

155
00:08:44,323 --> 00:08:45,966
Pause and try all five.

156
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,320
The warm-up deploys a small game,
tic-tac-toe, from one file with two objects.

157
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:57,568
The Deployment says what runs in a Pod,
how many copies, and to keep them running.

158
00:08:57,888 --> 00:09:01,536
The Service says how traffic reaches them,
and lets Pods die.

159
00:09:01,783 --> 00:09:04,556
The slide trims the lines
that glue them together.

160
00:09:04,876 --> 00:09:08,089
The template gives every Pod a label, run:
web;

161
00:09:08,089 --> 00:09:11,917
both objects find their Pods by that label,
not by name.

162
00:09:12,467 --> 00:09:17,630
Each Pod runs the image dlops/tic-tac-toe,
listening on port 8080.

163
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:21,789
The Service listens
on port 80 and forwards to 8080.

164
00:09:23,033 --> 00:09:26,852
Deploy the app:
kubectl apply -f, and the file.

165
00:09:27,172 --> 00:09:29,828
Apply only hands a wish to the API server;

166
00:09:29,828 --> 00:09:34,319
the scheduler picks two nodes,
and their kubelets start the containers.

167
00:09:34,867 --> 00:09:38,152
An extra check,
rollout status, watches it happen:

168
00:09:38,152 --> 00:09:43,080
zero, then one of two available,
then rolled out, on two different workers.

169
00:09:44,817 --> 00:09:47,164
Next, kubectl get services.

170
00:09:47,484 --> 00:09:50,951
The web Service is a load balancer:

171
00:09:50,951 --> 00:09:57,489
port 80, mapped to 31366 on every node,
and on to 8080 in the Pod.

172
00:09:57,809 --> 00:09:59,921
Its external IP says pending.

173
00:10:00,467 --> 00:10:03,027
43 seconds later it still says pending.

174
00:10:03,347 --> 00:10:06,504
Which part of the cluster picture
is waiting, and on what?

175
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:18,209
The controllers,
waiting on the cloud provider.

176
00:10:18,529 --> 00:10:22,527
On GKE they ask Google for a load balancer
and an address,

177
00:10:22,527 --> 00:10:24,801
which arrives after a short wait.

178
00:10:25,121 --> 00:10:27,681
The kind cluster has no cloud to ask.

179
00:10:28,417 --> 00:10:31,307
Pause: on GKE, wait for the external IP,

180
00:10:31,307 --> 00:10:34,774
copy it into your browser,
and the game appears.

181
00:10:36,017 --> 00:10:37,916
On the stand-in, an extra:

182
00:10:37,916 --> 00:10:41,350
port-forward to the Service, then curl:
200 OK.

183
00:10:41,670 --> 00:10:44,217
The lecture suggests it for your browser,

184
00:10:44,217 --> 00:10:48,689
but from the toolbox the port opens
inside the container: test it there.

185
00:10:49,933 --> 00:10:53,453
Back to the night-time problem:
who restarts the container?

186
00:10:53,773 --> 00:10:56,034
In Kubernetes, a controller does.

187
00:10:56,354 --> 00:10:57,784
Let's watch one work.

188
00:10:58,233 --> 00:11:00,281
An extra listing shows the chain.

189
00:11:00,601 --> 00:11:06,212
The Deployment named web owns a ReplicaSet,
whose name adds a hash of the Pod template.

190
00:11:06,532 --> 00:11:08,479
The ReplicaSet owns two Pods,

191
00:11:08,479 --> 00:11:11,566
each with that hash
and a short random suffix.

192
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:13,491
The labels confirm it:

193
00:11:13,491 --> 00:11:17,220
every Pod carries the template hash
and the run: web label.

194
00:11:17,466 --> 00:11:20,937
The ReplicaSet controller runs a loop:
observe the Pods,

195
00:11:20,937 --> 00:11:24,655
compare with the number you declared,
act on the difference.

196
00:11:24,975 --> 00:11:26,095
You set the number;

197
00:11:26,095 --> 00:11:28,922
the controller keeps it true,
like a thermostat.

198
00:11:29,466 --> 00:11:33,413
First, one more extra:
kubectl scale --replicas=4.

199
00:11:33,733 --> 00:11:36,558
Two more Pods appear,
spread over both workers,

200
00:11:36,558 --> 00:11:38,661
and the Service already lists them.

201
00:11:38,981 --> 00:11:44,485
Scale back to 2, and remember this command:
your project's Milestone 5 asks for it.

202
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:47,247
Now delete one of the two web Pods.

203
00:11:47,567 --> 00:11:50,039
A second later, how many web Pods are there,

204
00:11:50,039 --> 00:11:52,623
and which part of the new name
stays the same?

205
00:12:01,466 --> 00:12:02,466
Two.

206
00:12:02,533 --> 00:12:06,245
The delete returns,
and a new Pod is already being created.

207
00:12:06,565 --> 00:12:10,042
Its name keeps the ReplicaSet hash,
with a new suffix.

208
00:12:10,283 --> 00:12:12,480
15 seconds later, both run.

209
00:12:12,800 --> 00:12:15,872
Nobody logged in:
the loop noticed, and acted.

210
00:12:16,116 --> 00:12:19,068
That ticks failover,
with an honest footnote:

211
00:12:19,068 --> 00:12:21,364
it is a restart, not a hot standby.

212
00:12:21,684 --> 00:12:24,162
With one replica there is a short gap,

213
00:12:24,162 --> 00:12:27,487
which is why the game's file asks
for two replicas.

214
00:12:28,033 --> 00:12:32,193
In lecture 20, Docker restarted a container
on the same machine.

215
00:12:32,513 --> 00:12:35,990
A ReplicaSet places a new
Pod wherever there is room.

216
00:12:36,310 --> 00:12:38,700
Declare the result, don't script the steps.

217
00:12:39,950 --> 00:12:42,745
Pods are mortal:
the new Pod got a new address.

218
00:12:43,065 --> 00:12:44,814
So how does traffic find it?

219
00:12:45,134 --> 00:12:48,484
The Service keeps a list
of every Pod wearing its label,

220
00:12:48,484 --> 00:12:50,339
and that list follows the Pods.

221
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:55,065
An extra from a throwaway Pod:

222
00:12:55,065 --> 00:13:00,078
the name web.default.svc.cluster.local
points to the Service,

223
00:13:00,078 --> 00:13:03,693
and fetching just web returns the game page.

224
00:13:04,013 --> 00:13:06,040
The name stays while the Pods change.

225
00:13:06,283 --> 00:13:10,320
This is how the cheese app's API
will find its database:

226
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:12,555
by the Service name, vector-db.

227
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:16,704
Another extra:
kubectl exec into the web Deployment.

228
00:13:17,024 --> 00:13:21,312
Hostname prints the name of one Pod,
and the game's files are inside.

229
00:13:21,632 --> 00:13:24,619
The README shells into the API the same way.

230
00:13:24,867 --> 00:13:26,659
Why did get all look empty?

231
00:13:26,979 --> 00:13:29,560
Namespaces: folders for objects.

232
00:13:29,880 --> 00:13:33,063
Without a flag,
kubectl looks only in default;

233
00:13:33,063 --> 00:13:38,115
this extra lists them, then creates
and deletes the cheese-app-namespace.

234
00:13:38,733 --> 00:13:43,890
So in part 2, every command
about the app gets -n cheese-app-namespace,

235
00:13:43,890 --> 00:13:45,197
in the flags slot.

236
00:13:45,833 --> 00:13:51,124
Before deleting the cluster, an extra tip:
kubectl delete with the same file.

237
00:13:51,444 --> 00:13:56,372
On GKE, this releases the load balancer
while the cluster still exists.

238
00:13:56,617 --> 00:13:58,485
Then delete the cluster:

239
00:13:58,485 --> 00:14:03,465
gcloud container clusters delete
test-cluster --zone us-east1-c.

240
00:14:03,785 --> 00:14:06,601
Yours asks whether to continue; answer yes.

241
00:14:07,150 --> 00:14:08,729
We delete the stand-in too.

242
00:14:10,650 --> 00:14:14,170
The lecture's last word fits here:
don't forget to delete.

243
00:14:14,490 --> 00:14:18,351
Pause, check that your test cluster is gone,
and part 1 is done.

244
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:23,035
Part 2: the cheese app on GKE,
as Pulumi code.

245
00:14:23,355 --> 00:14:27,750
The course link lands on Deployment
with Scaling using Kubernetes;

246
00:14:27,750 --> 00:14:31,079
the cluster steps continue
under the next heading,

247
00:14:31,079 --> 00:14:32,677
Create & Deploy Cluster.

248
00:14:33,417 --> 00:14:36,724
Here is the plan,
piece by piece from lecture 20.

249
00:14:37,044 --> 00:14:39,902
Each long-running container
becomes a Deployment.

250
00:14:40,222 --> 00:14:42,513
The one-shot loader becomes a Job:

251
00:14:42,513 --> 00:14:47,433
it retries a failed run a few times,
and once it succeeds, it stays done.

252
00:14:47,967 --> 00:14:52,639
Container names on a Docker network
become Services with DNS names.

253
00:14:52,959 --> 00:14:57,213
The folders on the VM disk
become two persistent volume claims,

254
00:14:57,213 --> 00:14:59,103
each backed by a cloud disk.

255
00:14:59,350 --> 00:15:03,958
The nginx container becomes
an Ingress controller with an Ingress rule.

256
00:15:04,278 --> 00:15:08,758
The JSON key becomes Workload Identity,
with no key file at all.

257
00:15:09,078 --> 00:15:11,979
One machine becomes
a pool of one or two nodes.

258
00:15:12,217 --> 00:15:14,137
Around it, a private network.

259
00:15:14,457 --> 00:15:16,725
The nodes get no public addresses;

260
00:15:16,725 --> 00:15:22,329
a Cloud NAT gateway lets them reach out,
for example to pull Chroma from Docker Hub.

261
00:15:22,649 --> 00:15:26,446
The app sits in one namespace,
behind one load balancer.

262
00:15:28,050 --> 00:15:32,674
The program in deploy_k8s says the same
in four calls, one per file:

263
00:15:32,674 --> 00:15:36,754
the network, the cluster,
the containers, the load balancer.

264
00:15:37,074 --> 00:15:41,063
Line 17 keeps the HTTPS
variant switched off.

265
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:45,872
While it is up, it costs money:
the fee of a regional cluster,

266
00:15:45,872 --> 00:15:50,141
one or two nodes, a load balancer,
one NAT gateway and two disks.

267
00:15:50,461 --> 00:15:53,772
By our estimate,
roughly five to eight dollars a day:

268
00:15:53,772 --> 00:15:57,395
check the pricing calculator,
and destroy it the same day.

269
00:15:59,733 --> 00:16:03,186
This section enables the five APIs again;

270
00:16:03,186 --> 00:16:07,818
turn on the Vertex AI API too,
which the API Pods call.

271
00:16:08,566 --> 00:16:11,915
You also need the two service accounts
from lecture 20.

272
00:16:12,235 --> 00:16:15,222
The deployment account
does what the toolbox does,

273
00:16:15,222 --> 00:16:17,611
and here it is also the nodes' identity.

274
00:16:17,931 --> 00:16:22,796
The gcp-service account is for the API Pods,
with no key file.

275
00:16:23,533 --> 00:16:26,008
Two one-time steps the README leaves out.

276
00:16:26,328 --> 00:16:31,649
The deployment account very likely needs
Service Account Admin on gcp-service;

277
00:16:31,649 --> 00:16:33,218
we could not test this.

278
00:16:33,538 --> 00:16:35,984
And create the registry repository,

279
00:16:35,984 --> 00:16:39,128
cheese-app-repository,
which no code creates.

280
00:16:39,866 --> 00:16:43,118
The deployment key goes
in the secrets folder,

281
00:16:43,118 --> 00:16:45,946
beside the repository, as in lecture 20.

282
00:16:46,683 --> 00:16:50,159
Then the toolbox steps from part 1,
the same commands;

283
00:16:50,159 --> 00:16:54,406
if the toolbox is still running,
the script simply attaches to it.

284
00:16:54,650 --> 00:16:59,289
Next, the images stack,
only if you have not built it before:

285
00:16:59,289 --> 00:17:01,291
change into deploy_images,

286
00:17:01,291 --> 00:17:05,295
pulumi stack init dev,
and set gcp:project to yours.

287
00:17:05,615 --> 00:17:08,575
Ours says PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN must be set:

288
00:17:08,575 --> 00:17:13,295
our bucket login failed,
so Pulumi falls back to its cloud service.

289
00:17:13,533 --> 00:17:16,924
To show the rest,
we point Pulumi at a local folder,

290
00:17:16,924 --> 00:17:19,336
a recording workaround: don't run it.

291
00:17:19,656 --> 00:17:22,152
Now the stack is created, as yours is.

292
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:25,509
Then pulumi preview --stack dev:
three image builds,

293
00:17:25,509 --> 00:17:29,035
skipped in a preview,
and three tags stamped with the time.

294
00:17:29,355 --> 00:17:32,768
+ 4 to create:
three images and the stack itself.

295
00:17:33,016 --> 00:17:34,657
Those tag outputs matter:

296
00:17:34,657 --> 00:17:38,046
deploy_k8s looks them up
through a stack reference,

297
00:17:38,046 --> 00:17:39,907
to know which images to run.

298
00:17:40,150 --> 00:17:45,206
Last, pulumi up --stack dev -y:
it builds and pushes three images.

299
00:17:45,526 --> 00:17:50,215
Skip it if you did it in lecture 20,
with one condition we meet next;

300
00:17:50,215 --> 00:17:52,118
otherwise, pause and run it.

301
00:17:53,367 --> 00:17:55,330
Now Create & Deploy Cluster.

302
00:17:55,650 --> 00:17:57,560
From the deployment folder,

303
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:01,362
change into deploy_k8s
and run pulumi stack init dev:

304
00:18:01,362 --> 00:18:02,868
the same token error,

305
00:18:02,868 --> 00:18:06,124
and on the local backend,
Created stack 'dev'.

306
00:18:06,767 --> 00:18:09,480
Then three config keys: your project,

307
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:13,807
and two service account emails,
deployment and gcp-service.

308
00:18:14,127 --> 00:18:17,882
Replace ac215-project
with yours in all three.

309
00:18:19,384 --> 00:18:23,779
Line 9 takes the deployment email:
the nodes run as that account.

310
00:18:24,099 --> 00:18:29,709
Line 10 takes the gcp-service email,
which the API Pods will act as.

311
00:18:29,950 --> 00:18:33,388
An extra,
pulumi config, lists your three keys

312
00:18:33,388 --> 00:18:37,200
and two leftovers from the committed file,
setupSSL

313
00:18:37,200 --> 00:18:40,638
and an SSH user,
which the program never uses.

314
00:18:40,958 --> 00:18:44,777
A typo in a key name fails quietly later,
so check this list.

315
00:18:45,017 --> 00:18:48,345
Then Preview:
pulumi preview --stack dev --refresh.

316
00:18:48,665 --> 00:18:53,278
On the local backend, a Python error
at line 70 of setup_containers.py:

317
00:18:53,278 --> 00:18:55,812
'NoneType' object is not subscriptable.

318
00:18:56,132 --> 00:19:00,078
The program asked the images stack
for its tags, and got nothing.

319
00:19:00,317 --> 00:19:03,759
You see this
if the images stack never finished pulumi up

320
00:19:03,759 --> 00:19:05,330
in this same state bucket.

321
00:19:05,650 --> 00:19:08,509
Skipping it is safe
only when it already ran there.

322
00:19:08,750 --> 00:19:13,114
So we gave the program a stand-in images
stack with placeholder tags;

323
00:19:13,114 --> 00:19:14,190
nothing is built.

324
00:19:14,510 --> 00:19:16,446
Now the real plan appears:

325
00:19:16,446 --> 00:19:21,270
the network, the cluster,
the node pool, the Kubernetes objects,

326
00:19:21,270 --> 00:19:22,702
and + 23 to create.

327
00:19:22,950 --> 00:19:26,451
Two more lines are lookups,
not counted in the 23:

328
00:19:26,451 --> 00:19:29,393
the stack reference and the nginx Service.

329
00:19:29,713 --> 00:19:32,890
In the outputs,
only the cluster name is known;

330
00:19:32,890 --> 00:19:36,540
the address and the URL wait
until the cluster exists.

331
00:19:36,784 --> 00:19:38,663
Pause and run your preview:

332
00:19:38,663 --> 00:19:42,074
you should see the same 23,
with real image tags.

333
00:19:43,217 --> 00:19:47,100
Then the big one:
pulumi up --stack dev --refresh -y.

334
00:19:47,420 --> 00:19:51,345
On the local backend the update starts,
and the very first resource,

335
00:19:51,345 --> 00:19:55,270
the network, fails: invalid_grant:
Invalid grant: account not found.

336
00:19:55,517 --> 00:19:58,225
The network is the first thing
that needs Google,

337
00:19:58,225 --> 00:20:00,104
so from here on this is a picture.

338
00:20:00,424 --> 00:20:03,624
Pause and start yours;
from now on, the meter runs.

339
00:20:05,867 --> 00:20:08,000
Pulumi follows the dependencies.

340
00:20:08,320 --> 00:20:10,262
First the network and its subnet.

341
00:20:10,582 --> 00:20:11,819
Then, side by side,

342
00:20:11,819 --> 00:20:15,009
the router with its NAT gateway,
and the cluster,

343
00:20:15,009 --> 00:20:18,923
the long wait: a quarter of an hour or more,
by our estimate.

344
00:20:19,467 --> 00:20:20,747
Then the node pool.

345
00:20:21,067 --> 00:20:24,552
A Kubernetes provider,
built from the cluster outputs,

346
00:20:24,552 --> 00:20:28,747
waits for that pool,
so no Pod is created before there are nodes.

347
00:20:28,984 --> 00:20:32,820
Through it come the namespace,
the service account of the API

348
00:20:32,820 --> 00:20:37,538
and its binding, the two disk claims,
the frontend and the vector database.

349
00:20:37,784 --> 00:20:40,856
Next, the loader Job
fills the vector database.

350
00:20:41,176 --> 00:20:46,523
Pulumi creates the API
only after the Job completes:

351
00:20:46,523 --> 00:20:50,328
lines 319 to 321 set that dependency.

352
00:20:50,567 --> 00:20:54,343
In parallel,
Helm installs the nginx Ingress controller.

353
00:20:54,663 --> 00:20:57,077
Its Service asks for a load balancer,

354
00:20:57,077 --> 00:21:00,210
so the controllers ask the cloud
for an address:

355
00:21:00,210 --> 00:21:02,364
the part 1 lamp, green this time.

356
00:21:02,684 --> 00:21:06,204
That address becomes a host name,
and the Ingress comes last.

357
00:21:07,184 --> 00:21:08,976
Identity, in two parts.

358
00:21:09,296 --> 00:21:14,269
Pulumi and kubectl reach the API server
through the GKE auth plugin,

359
00:21:14,269 --> 00:21:18,512
fix two from the toolbox,
so there is no cluster password.

360
00:21:19,250 --> 00:21:23,362
And the API Pod wears
its Kubernetes service account,

361
00:21:23,362 --> 00:21:28,871
which Workload Identity swaps
for gcp-service at the door to Vertex AI.

362
00:21:29,191 --> 00:21:31,474
No JSON key sits in the cluster.

363
00:21:32,217 --> 00:21:35,287
Keep in mind: Pulumi connects kubectl once,

364
00:21:35,287 --> 00:21:39,001
inside this toolbox,
which is removed when you exit.

365
00:21:39,321 --> 00:21:41,348
The next session starts unconnected.

366
00:21:41,668 --> 00:21:45,087
If yours stops,
the card shows the four usual causes:

367
00:21:45,087 --> 00:21:46,830
pause and match your error.

368
00:21:57,367 --> 00:22:00,908
Lecture 20 put one nginx container in front.

369
00:22:01,228 --> 00:22:04,748
The game opened its own load balancer,
one door per app.

370
00:22:05,068 --> 00:22:08,972
The cheese app opens one door,
with a receptionist behind it.

371
00:22:09,517 --> 00:22:11,731
The README draws the path:

372
00:22:11,731 --> 00:22:16,246
browser, load balancer,
the nginx Ingress controller,

373
00:22:16,246 --> 00:22:20,081
then the frontend
on 3000 or the API on 9000,

374
00:22:20,081 --> 00:22:22,808
and the vector database on 8000.

375
00:22:23,050 --> 00:22:25,941
A small slip:
the README draws the dotted arrow

376
00:22:25,941 --> 00:22:28,341
from the vector database to the loader.

377
00:22:28,661 --> 00:22:32,458
The data flows the other way,
from the Job into the database.

378
00:22:32,700 --> 00:22:33,937
Follow one click.

379
00:22:34,257 --> 00:22:40,060
<IP>.sslip.io points back to that same IP,
with no setup at all.

380
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:45,415
The packet passes the cloud load balancer
and lands on the nginx controller.

381
00:22:46,050 --> 00:22:50,782
The Ingress rule starts with the host name:
for that host,

382
00:22:50,782 --> 00:22:57,229
/api-service goes to the API on 9000,
every other path to the frontend on 3000.

383
00:22:57,549 --> 00:23:00,124
The controller follows that rule,

384
00:23:00,124 --> 00:23:03,949
like the nginx.conf of lecture 20,
now generated.

385
00:23:04,484 --> 00:23:07,513
This time the prefix is kept:
nothing strips it.

386
00:23:07,833 --> 00:23:13,372
The API mounts itself under its root path,
/api-service,

387
00:23:13,372 --> 00:23:17,625
so /api-service/status answers version 3.0.

388
00:23:18,567 --> 00:23:21,166
The page comes from the frontend;

389
00:23:21,166 --> 00:23:25,735
then your browser calls the API directly,
at /api-service.

390
00:23:26,534 --> 00:23:31,724
Inside the cluster,
the API reaches Chroma by its Service name,

391
00:23:31,724 --> 00:23:33,702
vector-db, on port 8000.

392
00:23:34,500 --> 00:23:39,599
Now open the bare IP:
the same load balancer, without sslip.io.

393
00:23:39,919 --> 00:23:40,943
What answers?

394
00:23:50,084 --> 00:23:53,711
The nginx controller answers 404 Not Found.

395
00:23:54,031 --> 00:23:56,292
The rule matches only that host name,

396
00:23:56,292 --> 00:23:59,471
so the same door
with the wrong name finds no route.

397
00:23:59,791 --> 00:24:02,009
Use the app_url from the outputs.

398
00:24:02,550 --> 00:24:06,948
That ticks load balancing:
the cloud load balancer at the door,

399
00:24:06,948 --> 00:24:08,082
nginx behind it,

400
00:24:08,082 --> 00:24:11,766
and each Service spreading
requests across its Pods.

401
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,411
Next, try some kubectl commands.

402
00:24:15,731 --> 00:24:19,229
With no cluster here,
all five say connection refused.

403
00:24:19,549 --> 00:24:23,001
You will see exactly this
in any new toolbox session:

404
00:24:23,001 --> 00:24:26,973
the kubeconfig Pulumi wrote went away
with the old container.

405
00:24:27,217 --> 00:24:30,761
The fix is the command Pulumi ran,
an extra here:

406
00:24:30,761 --> 00:24:35,174
gcloud container clusters
get-credentials cheese-app-cluster.

407
00:24:35,494 --> 00:24:38,292
The code passes the region
to the --zone flag;

408
00:24:38,292 --> 00:24:40,785
write --region, which says what it means.

409
00:24:41,533 --> 00:24:45,539
Once connected, get all still shows
only the kubernetes Service,

410
00:24:45,539 --> 00:24:48,168
because the app sits in its own namespace.

411
00:24:48,488 --> 00:24:52,802
With -n cheese-app-namespace,
yours lists the frontend,

412
00:24:52,802 --> 00:24:58,685
database, API and nginx controller Pods,
and a loader Pod marked Completed.

413
00:24:59,005 --> 00:25:01,032
Get nodes shows one node, or two.

414
00:25:01,767 --> 00:25:04,135
The next README step shells into a Pod.

415
00:25:04,455 --> 00:25:07,311
The Pod name in the README
is the author's own;

416
00:25:07,311 --> 00:25:09,681
use the name your first command prints.

417
00:25:10,317 --> 00:25:13,397
Inside,
you get a shell in the API container,

418
00:25:13,397 --> 00:25:15,245
just as we did in the game.

419
00:25:15,565 --> 00:25:18,104
Print its environment
and look for ROOT_PATH.

420
00:25:18,424 --> 00:25:19,618
Pause and try it.

421
00:25:20,517 --> 00:25:25,360
To view the app, the README shows an outputs
block a little older than the code.

422
00:25:25,680 --> 00:25:30,099
Today the address is called ip_address,
not nginx_ingress_ip,

423
00:25:30,099 --> 00:25:33,359
and the Ingress
is called cheese-app-ingress.

424
00:25:34,100 --> 00:25:36,105
Pause and open your app_url.

425
00:25:36,425 --> 00:25:39,286
A footer that says API version:

426
00:25:39,286 --> 00:25:44,916
3.0 proves the whole path works:
it asks /api-service/status.

427
00:25:45,236 --> 00:25:50,655
Then pick the RAG model in the chat;
a reply proves Vertex AI and Chroma too.

428
00:25:50,900 --> 00:25:54,527
Right after deploying
you may see 502 or 503:

429
00:25:54,527 --> 00:25:57,428
the API is still loading TensorFlow.

430
00:25:57,748 --> 00:25:59,241
Wait, and try again.

431
00:26:00,483 --> 00:26:04,408
The README's debugging block
has 14 kubectl commands.

432
00:26:04,728 --> 00:26:06,247
As typed, they all fail;

433
00:26:06,247 --> 00:26:10,488
most say the namespace,
cheese-app-cluster-namespace, is not found.

434
00:26:10,733 --> 00:26:14,257
Lines with <pod-name>
are shell redirections:

435
00:26:14,257 --> 00:26:18,093
replace the whole placeholder,
brackets included.

436
00:26:18,833 --> 00:26:22,244
Two fixes:
the namespace is cheese-app-namespace,

437
00:26:22,244 --> 00:26:27,046
and the Job has one container,
so drop the -c wait-for-chromadb flag.

438
00:26:27,366 --> 00:26:28,625
Pause and try them.

439
00:26:29,366 --> 00:26:32,507
On a Job that exists,
an extra on the stand-in,

440
00:26:32,507 --> 00:26:35,446
the same commands work: done, one succeeded.

441
00:26:35,766 --> 00:26:38,156
Nothing replaces a finished Job's Pod.

442
00:26:38,900 --> 00:26:43,016
A small note:
the disk is mounted at /chroma/chroma,

443
00:26:43,016 --> 00:26:46,260
but today's Chroma image writes to /data.

444
00:26:46,580 --> 00:26:51,039
So the vectors live inside the container
and vanish when the Pod restarts.

445
00:26:51,359 --> 00:26:57,502
Change line 150 to /data,
or run the loader Job again after a restart.

446
00:27:00,650 --> 00:27:01,717
Now scaling.

447
00:27:02,037 --> 00:27:06,887
The lecture promises scaling
on CPU, memory or custom metrics,

448
00:27:06,887 --> 00:27:10,954
but the code sets no replicas
and no Pod autoscaler.

449
00:27:11,274 --> 00:27:14,154
It switches on the node layer:
one to two nodes.

450
00:27:14,900 --> 00:27:18,228
The scheduler packs Pods
onto nodes by their requests.

451
00:27:18,548 --> 00:27:22,196
The frontend asks for a quarter CPU
and 2 gigabytes.

452
00:27:22,516 --> 00:27:24,795
Chroma and nginx ask for little,

453
00:27:24,795 --> 00:27:28,425
and the API for nothing,
so nobody can plan for it.

454
00:27:29,066 --> 00:27:33,503
Scale the frontend to 3;
Milestone 5 asks you to show scaling.

455
00:27:33,823 --> 00:27:39,157
A copy that does not fit waits, pending,
until the autoscaler adds a second node.

456
00:27:39,477 --> 00:27:43,274
Ask for many more, and some stay pending:
the pool stops at two.

457
00:27:43,916 --> 00:27:47,287
The API's disk attaches
to one node at a time.

458
00:27:47,607 --> 00:27:52,705
Suppose you scaled the API to 3,
and some copies landed on the second node.

459
00:27:53,025 --> 00:27:56,162
What happens to them,
and can the autoscaler fix it?

460
00:28:07,300 --> 00:28:10,760
They stay stuck creating,
with a Multi-Attach error:

461
00:28:10,760 --> 00:28:12,889
the disk already sits on node 1.

462
00:28:13,209 --> 00:28:16,110
More nodes do not help;
the limit is the disk.

463
00:28:16,350 --> 00:28:21,974
That ticks scaling, in two levels:
nodes automatically, Pods in your hands;

464
00:28:21,974 --> 00:28:26,547
a Pod autoscaler on CPU needs requests,
and the API has none.

465
00:28:26,867 --> 00:28:28,744
Scale back to 1 when you are done.

466
00:28:29,983 --> 00:28:34,911
Last, delete the cluster:
pulumi destroy --stack dev --refresh -y.

467
00:28:35,231 --> 00:28:39,775
Ours has nothing real to delete;
yours deletes what this stack created,

468
00:28:39,775 --> 00:28:43,295
in reverse order,
from the Ingress down to the network.

469
00:28:43,916 --> 00:28:46,769
Then check what may survive, our suggestion:

470
00:28:46,769 --> 00:28:50,529
list clusters,
forwarding rules, disks and firewall rules.

471
00:28:50,849 --> 00:28:54,866
Those are deleted in the background
and can be left behind;

472
00:28:54,866 --> 00:28:56,567
look for disks named pvc.

473
00:28:56,816 --> 00:29:00,720
Registry images stay on purpose:
delete them in the console.

474
00:29:01,040 --> 00:29:04,304
Delete the lecture 20 machine too,
if it still runs.

475
00:29:04,624 --> 00:29:05,755
Pause and check.

476
00:29:06,016 --> 00:29:07,467
Back to the three asks.

477
00:29:07,787 --> 00:29:10,770
Failover: the ReplicaSet brings Pods back,

478
00:29:10,770 --> 00:29:14,037
and node auto repair
replaces broken machines.

479
00:29:14,357 --> 00:29:19,157
Load balancing: the cloud load balancer,
nginx and the Services.

480
00:29:19,477 --> 00:29:23,659
Scaling: the node autoscaler,
and kubectl scale in your hands.

481
00:29:23,900 --> 00:29:25,799
The lecture lists five payoffs.

482
00:29:26,119 --> 00:29:28,209
Self-service: one pulumi up.

483
00:29:28,529 --> 00:29:31,260
Lower cost: Pods packed by their requests.

484
00:29:31,580 --> 00:29:34,481
Automatic adjustment: the node autoscaler.

485
00:29:34,801 --> 00:29:36,785
Smooth running: self-healing.

486
00:29:37,105 --> 00:29:40,860
Simpler development:
the same images as lecture 20.

487
00:29:41,100 --> 00:29:42,209
Test yourself.

488
00:29:42,529 --> 00:29:46,011
Which control plane part decides
where a new Pod runs,

489
00:29:46,011 --> 00:29:48,460
and which one notices that a Pod died?

490
00:29:48,780 --> 00:29:51,849
Why does get all show only
the kubernetes Service,

491
00:29:51,849 --> 00:29:53,260
and what flag fixes it?

492
00:29:53,500 --> 00:29:56,132
How does the web Service know its Pods,

493
00:29:56,132 --> 00:29:59,303
and why does the API
reach Chroma at vector-db?

494
00:29:59,623 --> 00:30:03,762
Trace one click
from sslip.io to the API Pod;

495
00:30:03,762 --> 00:30:06,705
why does the bare IP answer 404?

496
00:30:06,950 --> 00:30:10,474
Why can the frontend scale to 3
but the API cannot,

497
00:30:10,474 --> 00:30:12,753
and what does the autoscaler add?

498
00:30:13,073 --> 00:30:16,251
Answers are in the exercises
and the study guide.

499
00:30:16,500 --> 00:30:19,812
Next, in lecture 23,
the two Pulumi programs,

500
00:30:19,812 --> 00:30:24,521
in version 4 of the app,
run from GitHub Actions on every merge.

